# Cloudflare Pages _headers — offline hardening (2026-10-27, rev2).
#
# Goal: ONLINE = always fresh, OFFLINE = the HTTP cache serves the last copy.
#
#  * max-age=0            => every request revalidates (updates land immediately)
#  * stale-if-error=1y    => if that revalidation FAILS (device offline), the
#                            browser serves the stale copy (RFC 5861)
#  * NO stale-while-revalidate on shell/code files: SWR would serve STALE code
#    bytes to online users (the very thing that left phones stuck on an old
#    sw.js). Code must be fresh online; only the ERROR path may be stale.
#
#  /sw.js is special: it must ALWAYS be the freshest bytes (no-store) so the
#    browser's service-worker update check detects new versions. A stale SW
#    script = phones silently stuck on an old offline layer.
#
#  Deliberately NOT listed: /wasm/pikafish.nnue* — the Cloudflare function
#    sets its own policy (max-age=0 + stale-while-revalidate + stale-iferror;
#    for the 50MB net, serving the cached copy instantly while revalidating in
#    the background is a feature, not a bug); the .part files keep the default
#    must-revalidate so a new engine deploy reassembles fresh bytes.

/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-if-error=31536000
/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-if-error=31536000
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/engine.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-if-error=31536000
/engine-wasm.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-if-error=31536000
/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-if-error=31536000
/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-if-error=31536000
/wasm/pikafish-single.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-if-error=31536000
/wasm/pikafish-single.wasm
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-if-error=31536000
